blocks.explanation:
Choke: a coil of thick copper wire wound closely in a large number of turns over a soft iron laminated core.

Model: a choke is modelled as a series RL circuit: a resistor R of very small value in series with an inductor L of large inductive reactance X_L = 2πfL. The inductance L is very high because of the high permeability of the iron core.

Why no power is lost: the power in a series RL circuit is P = I²R = VI cos θ, with tan θ = ωL/R. As R of a choke is negligibly small and ωL is large, θ is nearly 90° and the power factor cos θ is almost zero. So practically no power is dissipated as heat; the inductor stores energy in its magnetic field and returns it.

Uses:
(i) blocks high frequency AC while allowing DC and low frequency AC to pass;
(ii) filters high frequency AC noise from electronic circuits, giving cleaner DC output;
(iii) in switch-mode power supplies, helps regulate voltage and filter switching noise;
(iv) prevents unwanted RF signals leaking out of circuits;
(v) in fluorescent lights, generates transient voltages across the tube for the breakdown of the gas;
(vi) limits the rate of current change, protecting insulation from sudden surges.

In the impedance diagram, X_L = ωL and X_C = 1/ωC are directed opposite to each other, so the net reactance is X_L − X_C:
Z = √(R² + (X_L − X_C)²) ... (17.24)
Z = √(R² + (ωL − 1/ωC)²) ... (17.25)

High frequency: X_L ≫ X_C, the inductive reactance dominates and the circuit behaves as RL-series.
Low frequency: X_C ≫ X_L, the capacitive reactance dominates and it behaves as RC-series.

Resonance: in between there is a frequency ωᵣ at which X_L = X_C; the equal and opposite reactances cancel and the circuit behaves as a pure resistive circuit. Applying the condition:
X_L = X_C
ωᵣL = 1/ωᵣC
ωᵣ² = 1/LC
ωᵣ = 1/√LC; as ωᵣ = 2πfᵣ,
2πfᵣ = 1/√LC
fᵣ = 1/(2π√LC) ... (17.26)

Properties at resonance:
(i) impedance is minimum, Z = R;
(ii) current is maximum;
(iii) phase angle is zero, power factor 1;
(iv) the resistor gives damping; higher R, faster decay (like a car's shock absorber);
(v) used for tuning in radio receivers and as band-pass filters;
(vi) voltage amplification: V across L and C can be much greater than the applied voltage;
(vii) true power P = VI cos θ (17.27) is maximum when θ = 0.
